<!--
  Intraday SalesRabbit sync. Every 5 minutes, all day.

  The crawl is an incremental watermark pull (~45 leads per 5 min); the fixed
  cost of a tick is the recompile. That recompile was 12s when this interval was
  set and is not anymore — the store grew past 450 MB and a FULL compile now
  costs minutes and a couple of GB of heap on an 8 GB machine, which is what
  started killing ticks at the watchdog on 2026-08-18. run-grow-sync.sh passes
  --scheduled, which since then means --lean: the three big CSVs nobody reads on
  a tick are skipped and a tick is back to well under a minute. See the LEAN
  comment in scripts/grow-sync.mjs.

  run-grow-sync.sh holds a pid lock and kills any run past 4 minutes, so a slow
  tick can never collide with the next one.

  If you change this, change `every:` and `sched:` on the 'sr' row in
  index.html (_growSyncStatus) too — the health board measures "runs missed"
  against that number, and if they drift it will call a healthy feed late.

  StartInterval rather than a list of hours: with no window to stay inside,
  drift doesn't matter, and an interval is what handles sleep well. launchd
  COALESCES missed interval firings — close the lid for four hours and you get
  ONE catch-up run on wake, not the two dozen you slept through. The sync's
  watermark means that single run covers the whole gap anyway.

  Runs are cheap and silent: no browser, just HTTP against apis.salesrabbit.com
  using the token seeded by scripts/grow-sync.mjs --seed.

  Install:
    cp scripts/launchd/com.grow.sr-sync.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.grow.sr-sync.plist
  Check:   launchctl list | grep sr-sync
  Remove:  launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.grow.sr-sync.plist
-->
